{"seconds": 150.9, "exit": 0, "final": "This doesn't seem like a forced win either. But at least after Bb8 Rd8, White plays Rxd8+ and wins the rook. That might be the key idea!\n\nSo the move is Bb8, and the main line is:\n1. Bb8! and if Black plays:\n   - Kxb8, then 2. Rd8+ with a forking attack\n   - Rd8, then 2. Rxd8+ winning the rook\n   - Bxa6 or Bxe5, then 2. Rd8+ with checks and tactical play\n\nThe key motif is that Bb8 FORCES the Black king or rook to make a critical decision, leading to tactical wins. The most forcing continuation seems to be:\n\n1. Bb8 Rd8 (or Kxb8) 2. Rxd8+ (or Rd8+ after Kxb8)\n\nLet me double-check by setting up the main line:\n\n1. Bb8 - main line is Black's best try\n\nIf 1...Rd8, then 2. Rxd8+ Kxd8 and White has won the rook.\n\nIf 1...Kxb8, then 2. Rd8+ and the rook forks king and rook on h8.\n\nLet me verify which is more forcing. Actually, 1...Rd8 2. Rxd8+ wins the rook immediately. That seems like the clearest win!\n\nSo: **Bb8** is the best move.\n\nMain line: **1. Bb8 Rd8 2. Rxd8+ Kxd8** and White is winning (won the rook for bishop).\n\nBut wait, let me make sure this is better than Black's other options after Bb8. If Black plays 1...Bxa6, then 2. Rd8+ Kb7 3. Rd7+ and the king is being hunted. Is that also winning?\n\nThe motif is **fork** (Bb8 threatens the a7 pawn and controls the king's escape, forcing the rook to d8 where it's taken) or more specifically, it's about **attraction** or **deflection** - forcing the rook to d8 where it can be captured.\n\nActually, the key insight is that Bb8 ATTACKS a7, which is currently undefended. The Black king on c8 must deal with this threat. If it takes the bishop (Kxb8), then Rd8+ forks. If Black tries to defend with Rd8, then Rxd8+ wins the rook.\n\nLet me write up the solution:\n\n**Best move: Bb8**\n\n**Main line:** 1. Bb8! (threatening a7) Rd8 (if Kxb8, then Rd8+ forks king and rook) 2. Rxd8+ Kxd8 and White has won the exchange or more material.\n\n**Motif: Fork** (the rook forks king and Black's rook after the forcing sequence)\n\nActually, I should verify that this is indeed the \"one clearly best move\" by checking if there are other moves that might be just as good. But based on my analysis, Bb8 seems to force a winning continuation.\n\nLet me compose the coaching note:\n\n\"After Bb8, you attack the undefended a7 pawn and force Black's hand. If Black's rook comes to d8 to help defend, you win it with Rxd8+. If the king takes your bishop with Kxb8, Rd8+ creates a fork, winning the rook on h8. This forces your opponent into a losing position through concrete tactics\u2014always look for moves that attack weak pieces or pawns while controlling key squares.\"\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Bb8\", \"line\": [\"Bb8\", \"Rd8\", \"Rxd8+\", \"Kxd8\"], \"motifs\": [\"fork\", \"attraction\"]}\n```", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "2k4r/p5pp/P2Bp3/1b2Pp2/2p1p3/8/4B1Pb/3R1K2 w - - 0 27"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "2k4r/p5pp/P2Bp3/1b2Pp2/2p1p3/8/4B1Pb/3R1K2 w - - 0 27"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "2k4r/p5pp/P2Bp3/1b2Pp2/2p1p3/8/4B1Pb/3R1K2 w - - 0 27"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "2k4r/p5pp/P2Bp3/1b2Pp2/2p1p3/8/4B1Pb/3R1K2 w - - 0 27", "move": "Bf8"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "2k4r/p5pp/P2Bp3/1b2Pp2/2p1p3/8/4B1Pb/3R1K2 w - - 0 27", "move": "Be7"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "2k4r/p5pp/P2Bp3/1b2Pp2/2p1p3/8/4B1Pb/3R1K2 w - - 0 27", "move": "Bb8"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "2k4r/p5pp/P2Bp3/1b2Pp2/2p1p3/8/4B1Pb/3R1K2 w - - 0 27", "move": "Rd8"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "2k4r/p5pp/P2Bp3/1b2Pp2/2p1p3/8/4B1Pb/3R1K2 w - - 0 27", "move": "Bc7"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "2k4r/p5pp/P2Bp3/1b2Pp2/2p1p3/8/4B1Pb/3R1K2 w - - 0 27", "move": "Bc5"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_mo